const QUESTIONS = [
  // ===================================================================
  // Part I: Prices and Markets
  // ===================================================================
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Sowell defines economics as the study of…', 'The use of scarce resources which have alternative uses', ['How businesses and households make and spend money', 'How governments manage growth, jobs and inflation', 'How wealth is produced and how it should be shared'],
    'Money is just one way of measuring it. The core of economics is choosing among alternative uses of things that are scarce.', 'It isn’t really about money. Think about what every society is short of, and what it has to choose between.'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Can a decision be “economic” if no money is involved?', 'Yes: any choice about using scarce resources is economic', ['No: without money there are no prices, so no economics', 'Only if it affects a market where goods are bought and sold', 'Only once someone puts a money value on what’s chosen'],
    'Time, labor and attention are scarce too. Deciding how to use them is an economic decision, cash or no cash.', 'Your time is limited, and you can only spend each hour once.'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Would a primitive society with no money, or a commune where nobody charges anyone, have any costs?', 'Yes: costs are the alternatives given up, money or not', ['No: costs only exist once things have prices to pay', 'Only the costs of goods bought from outsiders with money', 'No, because sharing removes costs along with the prices'],
    'A cost is a foregone opportunity. Money just puts a number on it; it doesn’t create it.', 'If you spend the day fishing, what didn’t you do?'),
  q(1, 4, 'hard', 'Why is it a problem when a government sets official “national priorities”?', 'No goal is worth more than everything else at every margin', ['Politicians pick priorities to win votes, not to help people', 'Priorities should be set by voters directly, not officials', 'It isn’t; ranking goals is simply good planning'],
    'Some water is worth more than any diamond, but not every extra gallon. Fixed priorities pour resources into one goal long past the point where it’s worth it.', 'Is the tenth gallon of water worth as much to you as the first?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Soviet factories were judged by whether they met numerical production targets. What did that tend to produce?', 'Whatever hit the numbers, wanted or not', ['Exactly what planners knew people needed', 'Fewer goods, but of much higher quality', 'Cheap goods that sold out immediately'],
    'Judge output by weight and you get heavy, useless goods; judge it by count and you get flimsy ones. Without prices, nobody asks what consumers actually want.', 'People produce whatever they’re rewarded for producing.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'The Soviet economy used more steel and energy per unit of output than market economies. Why?', 'Without profit and loss, managers had no reason to economize', ['Heavy industry needs more steel per unit than consumer goods', 'The cold climate and vast distances used up extra energy', 'Soviet plants were newer and built on a much larger scale'],
    'In a market, wasting inputs eats into profits. Under central planning, using more resources cost the manager nothing.', 'Who lost anything if a Soviet factory wasted materials?'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'The Soviet Union often had shortages and unsold surpluses at the same time. How?', 'Planners’ prices didn’t adjust, so goods piled up in the wrong places', ['Transport was too poor to move goods from farms to the cities', 'Consumers saved their money instead of spending it on goods', 'Factories produced for export first and the home market second'],
    'Moving prices are what tell producers to make more of one thing and less of another. Freeze them and the signals stop.', 'In a market, what moves when goods pile up or run short?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'How can a rise in the demand for paper raise the price of baseball bats?', 'Both use wood, so paper makers bid up the price of wood', ['Bat makers use more paper for packaging and paperwork', 'Paper and sporting goods are often sold by the same stores', 'It can’t: paper and baseball bats are separate markets'],
    'Scarce resources have alternative uses. Paper, furniture and bats all compete for the same wood.', 'What raw material do paper and baseball bats have in common?'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'More demand for cheese can make catchers’ mitts more expensive. Why?', 'Dairies keep more cows alive, so there’s less cowhide', ['Dairies buy up leather to make equipment for their cows', 'Higher cheese prices push up prices in the whole economy', 'Leather workers move into cheese making for higher pay'],
    'Dairies keep more cows alive to milk them, so fewer go to slaughter, cowhide gets scarcer and leather goods cost more.', 'Cheese comes from milk, milk comes from cows, and leather comes from…'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'What’s the difference between scarcity and a shortage?', 'Scarcity is a fact of life; a shortage is a price problem', ['Scarcity is a short-term problem; a shortage lasts for years', 'A shortage is just a bigger, more serious kind of scarcity', 'Scarcity affects poor countries; shortages affect rich ones'],
    'Scarcity is a fact of life everywhere. A shortage is a price problem, usually a price held below what the market would set.', 'One exists everywhere, always. The other depends on what the price is.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'Under rent control, can a housing shortage grow even while the amount of housing per person rises?', 'Yes: low rents make people want more space than before', ['No: a growing shortage needs fewer homes per person', 'Only if the population is growing faster than housing', 'Only if landlords take apartments off the market'],
    'A growing shortage without growing scarcity: rent control made people take more space, so “not enough housing” got worse as housing grew.', 'When something gets cheaper, people want more of it, even space.'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Can there be a food surplus in a country where people are hungry?', 'Yes: prices held above the market leave food unsold', ['No: hungry people would always buy up any surplus', 'Only if the food spoils before it reaches the cities', 'Only where the government owns all of the farms'],
    'In 1930s America, farm produce was plowed under and milk poured down sewers to hold prices up, while children suffered from malnutrition.', 'What happens when the price is set higher than people will pay?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'A housing shortage vanishes almost overnight, before any new homes could have been built. What probably happened?', 'Rent control ended, so people made do with less space', ['Thousands of tenants moved away to cheaper cities', 'The government quietly converted offices into homes', 'Landlords were ordered to rent out all empty units'],
    'When rents rise to market levels, people economize on space, and the existing housing goes further.', 'What changes how much space people choose to rent, faster than builders can build?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'After rent control ends, what usually happens in the longer run?', 'More housing gets built, now that it pays to build', ['Rents keep rising until most tenants are priced out', 'Builders wait for the controls to come back', 'Landlords sell up and housing stock shrinks'],
    'Higher rents are the signal that tells builders to build. That’s how the shortage goes away for good.', 'Higher rents send a signal. Who responds to it?'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'A price ceiling holds a product’s price below the market level. What happens to the quantity demanded?', 'It rises', ['It falls', 'It stays the same', 'It drops to zero'],
    'Cheaper things get bought in larger quantities. That’s the first half of every shortage.', 'Cheaper things get bought in larger amounts.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'Under a price ceiling below the market level, what happens to the quantity supplied?', 'It falls', ['It rises', 'It stays the same', 'It doubles'],
    'Producers make less of what pays less. More wanted plus less offered equals a shortage.', 'Would you produce more of something if you were paid less for it?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'What tends to happen to the quality of price-controlled goods?', 'It declines, as sellers cut quality instead of raising prices', ['It rises, as sellers compete for customers on quality instead', 'It stays the same, since quality standards are set by law', 'It improves, because controlled goods sell out so quickly'],
    'In a shortage, customers take what they can get, so sellers have little reason to keep up quality.', 'If you can’t raise the price, what else can you change?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Which of these does a price ceiling below the market price NOT tend to cause?', 'A surplus', ['Black markets', 'Hoarding', 'Fewer of the services that usually come with the product'],
    'Price ceilings cause shortages. It’s price floors, set above the market, that cause surpluses.', 'Ceilings and floors have opposite effects.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'During the 1970s gasoline price controls, what happened to services like checking the oil and washing windshields?', 'They were cut back: stations sold out without them', ['They became free, to make up for the long lines', 'They were made compulsory by federal regulators', 'They improved, as stations competed for scarce drivers'],
    'With more customers than gasoline, stations could cut hours and services and still sell out.', 'If drivers line up anyway, why bother to win them over?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'After rent control laws are passed, how do builders tend to use bricks, pipes and labor?', 'More goes into luxury housing and offices, often exempt from controls', ['More goes into ordinary housing, now that the demand for it is higher', 'More goes into repairing existing buildings, since tenants stay longer', 'Nothing changes, since builders and landlords are different people'],
    'Resources flow to where they earn the most. Controlled ordinary housing earns less, so it gets less.', 'Resources go where they earn the most. What’s left uncontrolled?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Under rent control, what happens to the average number of people per apartment?', 'It falls: people keep more space than they need', ['It rises, as families crowd into cheap apartments', 'It stays the same, since tenants rarely move', 'It rises, since landlords split up large apartments'],
    'In rent-controlled San Francisco, about half the apartments had only one occupant.', 'Would you give up a big apartment if it was cheap?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Under rent control, how long do tenants tend to stay in the same apartment?', 'Longer: nobody gives up a cheap controlled flat', ['Shorter: landlords push tenants out more often', 'The same as before, since needs don’t change', 'Shorter: people move up once they save on rent'],
    'Tenants stay put even when their needs change, so fewer apartments come free for newcomers.', 'What would you lose by moving out?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'What does rent control do to the quality and average age of housing?', 'Quality falls and buildings get older', ['Quality rises, as tenants stay and care for them', 'Buildings get newer, as old ones are replaced', 'No effect: quality depends on housing codes'],
    'With a waiting line of tenants, landlords don’t need to maintain buildings, and few new ones get built.', 'If tenants are lining up, does the landlord need to fix the roof?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Why do price controls cause a worse shortage of gasoline than of strawberries?', 'Gasoline can be hoarded in car tanks; strawberries rot', ['People need gasoline more than they need strawberries', 'Gasoline comes from abroad, so supply can’t adjust', 'Strawberry farmers can grow more within a season'],
    'Controls cause hoarding wherever goods keep. Perishable goods and services, like haircuts, can’t be stockpiled.', 'Which one can you stockpile?'),
  q(1, 4, 'easy', 'Are prices usually higher or lower in low-income neighborhoods?', 'Higher, because costs there are higher', ['Lower, because people there can afford less', 'Higher, because stores there face no competition', 'Lower, because rents and wages there are cheaper'],
    'Prices reflect costs. It costs more to run a store, or lend money, where losses are more likely.', 'Think about crime, insurance and how fast goods sell.'),
  q(1, 4, 'medium', 'Why do check-cashing services charge more than banks charge their customers?', 'Each check is riskier and costlier, since customers have no bank records', ['They face no competition in poor areas, so they charge what they like', 'Banks are subsidized by the government, so they can afford to charge less', 'They target people who don’t understand fees, which regulators allow'],
    'A bank knows its account holders already. A check casher is taking a chance on a stranger every time.', 'A bank already knows its customers. Who does a check casher know?'),
  q(1, 4, 'hard', 'When a government program backfires, does that prove its officials are irrational or incompetent?', 'No: they may be responding rationally to their incentives', ['Yes: rational officials would have foreseen the results', 'Only if they ignored warnings from economists', 'Yes: backfiring programs show who’s unqualified'],
    'Officials are judged by different things than businesses are. Rational people in a badly designed system produce bad results.', 'People respond to the incentives they face, in government too.'),

  // ===================================================================
  // Part II: Industry and Commerce
  // ===================================================================
  q(2, 7, 'medium', 'Why would a corporation pay millions to get rid of a CEO who has failed?', 'Keeping a failing CEO can cost billions', ['Boards protect their friends at stockholders’ expense', 'Contracts signed in good times are legally binding', 'It avoids bad publicity that would hurt the stock'],
    'Like a divorce, it can cost more than the wedding, but delays can cost far more than the “golden parachute.”', 'Compare the cost of the payout with the cost of keeping him.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Toyota kept only hours’ worth of parts on hand; Soviet factories kept almost a year’s worth. Why?', 'Toyota’s suppliers were reliable; Soviet suppliers weren’t', ['Soviet planners stockpiled parts in case of a war', 'Toyota’s factories were too small to store more parts', 'Soviet factories made far more cars, so needed more parts'],
    'Japanese suppliers often delivered three or four times a day. Soviet economists said their inventories nearly equaled a year’s output.', 'Would you keep a year’s supply of something you could count on getting tomorrow?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Why are huge inventories a handicap, not an advantage?', 'They use up resources without raising anyone’s living standards', ['They’re taxed as assets, which raises the costs of production', 'They go out of date quickly, as products change every year', 'They aren’t: big inventories protect against every shortage'],
    'Inventory costs money and earns nothing. Soviet economists called it a heavier weight than capitalist recessions.', 'Goods sitting in a warehouse cost money. What are they doing for anyone?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Why did big Chinese firms long transport their own goods instead of hiring transport companies?', 'State transport firms had no reason to deliver on time or with care', ['Chinese law required each enterprise to own its own trucks', 'Owning trucks was cheaper than paying a transport company', 'It created jobs, which was the main goal of state enterprises'],
    'A TV maker might move its sets less efficiently, but it wouldn’t smash them. China ended up spending about twice the US share of income on transport.', 'Would you trust your TV sets to a carrier that lost nothing if it broke them?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'Why did mail-order houses like Montgomery Ward and Sears thrive in the late 1800s?', 'Most people lived in rural areas, where mail order was cheaper', ['Rural stores were banned from selling city goods by law', 'People preferred catalogs to the service in local shops', 'The post office delivered for free to rural customers'],
    'With railroad tracks running through their Chicago warehouses, they undersold rural stores, driving many out of business.', 'Where did most Americans live in the late 1800s?'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'Robert Wood saw that urban department stores would beat mail order. What happened to him at Montgomery Ward?', 'He was fired, joined Sears, and Sears built stores first', ['He was promoted, and Ward led the move into stores', 'He was ignored, but stayed on until he retired', 'He left to found J.C. Penney with his own money'],
    'Meanwhile J.C. Penney, who started with little money, built over a thousand stores by 1930. Ward followed late and never caught up.', 'Being right early isn’t always popular with the boss.'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', '“The market exacts a price for inertia.” What happened to Montgomery Ward after it clung to mail order?', 'It lost ground to Sears and never caught up', ['Its loyal catalog customers kept it on top', 'It thrived by avoiding risky new stores', 'It merged with Sears to stay in business'],
    'Insight beat size: those who read the changing country right overtook giants who didn’t.', 'Its rival changed with the country. It didn’t.'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'When Sewell Avery stepped down as Montgomery Ward’s chief executive, what did its stock do?', 'It rose immediately', ['It fell sharply', 'It didn’t move', 'Trading was suspended'],
    'He’d hoarded so much cash that Fortune called the company “a bank with a store front,” while Sears used its money to expand.', 'Stockholders had been unhappy with how he ran the company.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'General Motors buys its tires; Soviet firms made their own parts, sometimes even their own bricks. Why?', 'Soviet suppliers were unreliable, so firms did it all themselves', ['Soviet firms were bigger, so making parts in-house was cheaper', 'GM is barred by anti-trust law from making its own tires', 'Planners wanted each firm to be self-sufficient in wartime'],
    'Specializing only pays when you can count on other specialists to deliver.', 'Specializing only pays if you can count on other specialists.'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'In a market economy, what job do losses do?', 'They force a stop to using resources in ways people don’t value', ['None: losses are just bad luck that profits make up for later', 'They show which owners were too greedy and charged too much', 'They tell the government which industries to subsidize'],
    'Profits pull resources toward what people want; losses push them away from what people don’t. Both are signals.', 'Profits pull resources somewhere. Losses do the opposite.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Soviet tractor drivers were paid by the area they plowed. What did they do?', 'Plowed deep at the edges and shallow in the middle', ['Plowed extra slowly and carefully to avoid mistakes', 'Worked night and day to plow the biggest area', 'Plowed only the best land and skipped the rest'],
    'Plan fulfillment was counted in hectares worked, so covering ground fast beat plowing well.', 'They were paid for hectares covered, not for how well.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'If those tractor drivers had been plowing their own farms, what would likely change?', 'They’d plow properly, and farms would be smaller', ['Nothing: workers do the same job whoever owns the land', 'Farms would grow larger, to buy more tractors', 'They’d plow less, since no one could make them work'],
    'Huge collective farms had diseconomies of scale: too big to monitor. Owners have every reason to do the work right.', 'Owners bear the cost of bad work themselves.'),
  q(2, 7, 'medium', 'What made it possible to pool the savings of millions of people to build railroads?', 'Corporations with limited liability', ['Government ownership of the railroads', 'Laws requiring citizens to buy bonds', 'Banks lending with no risk to depositors'],
    'Without limited liability, buying one share could put your house at risk, and few would invest in ventures they can’t watch.', 'Would you invest if one share could cost you your house?'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Advertising is often said to benefit only the advertiser. Is that right?', 'No: it informs consumers and can bring prices down', ['Yes: consumers pay for it in higher prices', 'Yes: it only persuades people to buy what they don’t need', 'Only when it’s for a new product nobody knows about'],
    'Advertising spreads information people would otherwise spend time and money to find, and brands put a reputation on the line.', 'How would you find out about products without it?'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why do retired people get such cheap rates on cruises?', 'They can sail off-season, filling cabins that would be empty', ['Laws require seniors’ discounts on most forms of travel', 'Cruise lines expect them to spend more money on board', 'They book years ahead, so cruise lines can plan better'],
    'Off-season passengers cost little extra, since the ship sails anyway. Retirees aren’t tied to work vacations or school calendars.', 'A ship that sails half empty costs almost as much to run as a full one.'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'Why is the old dream of “eliminating the middleman” always frustrated?', 'Someone still has to do the job, and middlemen do it cheapest', ['Middlemen have enough political influence to block reforms', 'Laws protect middlemen from producers selling direct', 'Consumers prefer to deal with someone they already know'],
    'If you could do the middleman’s job cheaper yourself, you already would.', 'If you could do the middleman’s job cheaper, why aren’t you?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'The A & P grocery chain cut its profit margins, and its rate of profit on investment rose. How?', 'Faster sales let the same capital turn over many more times', ['It made up the difference by cutting its workers’ wages', 'Its suppliers had to give it bigger discounts to keep up', 'Lower margins meant lower taxes on the company’s profits'],
    'A small profit on each sale, made many more times a year, can beat a big profit on fewer sales.', 'A small profit, made many times a year, can add up to more.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why might a luxury hotel charge less than a budget hotel nearby?', 'Budget hotels fill first, so a dip in tourism empties luxury ones', ['Luxury hotels get tax breaks that budget hotels can’t get', 'Luxury hotels make their money on restaurants, not rooms', 'Budget hotels have more costs per room than luxury ones'],
    'In Cancún, a Best Western was $180 a night while the nearby Ritz-Carlton was $169. One was full, the other wasn’t.', 'Who books early, and who gets the empty rooms when fewer tourists come?'),
  q(2, 8, 'medium', 'What’s the difference between protecting competition and protecting competitors?', 'Protecting competitors shields inefficient firms at consumers’ cost', ['Protecting competitors keeps prices down by keeping more firms open', 'None: competition can’t exist without protecting the competitors', 'Protecting competition means keeping foreign firms out of the market'],
    'Competition means the efficient win customers. Rules that save the losers waste scarce resources.', 'One protects a process; the other protects particular firms from it.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Stores in low-income areas charge more to cover higher costs. What stops them charging enough to earn normal profits?', 'Higher prices send customers to other neighborhoods', ['Price controls in poor areas cap what they can charge', 'Unions in those areas push up wages faster than prices', 'Nothing: they earn more than stores in richer areas'],
    'Prices can only rise so far before shoppers go elsewhere, so these stores tend to earn less, and fewer open.', 'Customers can always shop somewhere else.'),
  q(2, 9, 'medium', 'In a market economy, what coordinates millions of producers and consumers who’ve never met?', 'Prices, which carry information about scarcity', ['Industry associations that share sales forecasts', 'Government statistics on supply and demand', 'Big companies that plan for their whole industries'],
    'Nobody needs to know why copper got scarcer. The higher price tells everyone to use less of it.', 'Nobody needs to know why copper got scarce to use less of it.'),
  // ===================================================================
  // Part III: Work and Pay
  // ===================================================================
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'As machines replaced human muscle, what became more valuable at work?', 'Knowledge, skills and experience', ['Physical stamina and endurance', 'Seniority and union protection', 'Willingness to work long hours'],
    'Industrialization shrank the advantage of brute strength and made experience pay, which also opened more doors to women.', 'If a machine does the lifting, what’s left for the worker to bring?'),
  q(3, 10, 'hard', 'Peak earnings now come later in life, and are a bigger multiple of beginners’ pay. What does that mean for women who take years off to raise children?', 'They lose more ground, as experience counts for more', ['They catch up faster, as skills matter more than strength', 'Nothing changes, since equal pay laws close the gap', 'They gain, since time out builds other useful skills'],
    'When experience is worth more, gaps in experience cost more. Much of the male-female earnings gap tracks that.', 'If experience pays more than ever, what do gaps in experience cost?'),
  q(3, 10, 'hard', 'Would the average factory hammer drive more nails a year in a richer country or a poorer one?', 'A poorer country, where scarce tools are used more', ['A richer country, where workers are more productive', 'The same everywhere, since a hammer is a hammer', 'A richer country, where factories run longer shifts'],
    'Where capital is scarce, each tool is worked harder; where it’s plentiful, tools sit idle more often.', 'Where is each tool shared by more workers?'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Who produces more output per hour: the average worker in a richer country or in a poorer one?', 'In a richer country, with more capital per worker', ['In a poorer country, where people must work harder', 'About the same everywhere, for the same kind of job', 'In a poorer country, with fewer rules slowing work'],
    'Output per worker depends heavily on what they work with. That’s also why pay is higher there.', 'What workers have to work with matters as much as how hard they work.'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Can income per person rise 50 percent while average household income stays flat?', 'Yes, if households are getting smaller', ['No: household and personal income move together', 'Only if inflation eats up the household’s gains', 'Only if higher taxes take the household’s share'],
    'Fewer people per household means each household’s income is shared by fewer people. Household figures can hide rising living standards.', 'What’s happening to the number of people in each household?'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Is income inequality greater in a single year, or over people’s whole lives?', 'In a single year, since people move between brackets over time', ['Over whole lifetimes, since advantages build up year after year', 'It’s the same either way, since most people keep their bracket', 'Over whole lifetimes, since the rich get richer as they age'],
    'The young beginner and the peak-earning veteran are often the same person at different ages.', 'The student and the senior executive may be the same person, decades apart.'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Why do statistics about “the poor” include many people who aren’t really poor?', 'They include students and beginners whose low income is temporary', ['Many people underreport their incomes to qualify for benefits', 'They count wealth rather than income, which misleads', 'They include retirees with pensions that aren’t counted'],
    'A bracket is a snapshot. Many in the bottom bracket this year will be in higher ones later.', 'Who earns little this year but much more later?'),
  q(3, 10, 'hard', '“A growing share of income goes to the top 10 percent.” In what sense is that misleading?', 'The top bracket isn’t the same people over time', ['It leaves out the effect of inflation on incomes', 'It counts salaries but leaves out business income', 'It measures income before taxes and transfers'],
    'It’s true of statistical categories, not of flesh-and-blood people, who move between brackets over the years.', 'Is a bracket the same group of people from one year to the next?'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'If unionized city bus drivers are paid above the market rate, what tends to happen to the buses?', 'They get bigger and come less often', ['They get smaller and come more often', 'Fares rise but service stays the same', 'More drivers get hired to share the work'],
    'When drivers cost more, transit systems economize on drivers: bigger buses, longer waits.', 'If drivers cost more, how would you use fewer of them?'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'How can bad roads or corruption lower the value of a country’s labor?', 'They cut the value of what workers produce, so pay is lower', ['They make workers less healthy, so they work fewer hours', 'They drive skilled workers abroad, leaving only the unskilled', 'They don’t: labor is worth the same wherever it’s done'],
    'A worker’s pay reflects the value of the output, and that depends on getting goods to market and keeping what you earn.', 'Pay reflects the value of what gets produced, and delivered, and kept.'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Why isn’t a worker’s productivity the same thing as their merit?', 'It also depends on equipment, management and co-workers', ['Productivity only counts hours worked, not effort or skill', 'Merit is about character, which pay can never measure', 'It is the same thing, measured over a long enough time'],
    'A great hitter in a weak lineup sees fewer good pitches. A skilled worker with poor tools produces less.', 'A great hitter in a weak lineup sees fewer good pitches.'),
  q(3, 11, 'easy', 'What does a minimum wage set above many workers’ productivity tend to do?', 'Price low-skilled workers out of jobs', ['Raise the incomes of all low-paid workers', 'Make employers more productive to cover costs', 'Create a shortage of workers to fill jobs'],
    'An employer won’t keep paying someone more than their work brings in. The least experienced are hit hardest.', 'Would you pay someone more than their work brings in?'),
  q(3, 11, 'hard', 'Studies surveyed firms before and after a minimum wage rise, then added up their jobs. What’s wrong with that method?', 'Firms that closed aren’t in the second survey', ['Firms exaggerate their hiring to look good', 'Too few firms answer for a reliable sample', 'Nothing: it’s the standard, sound method'],
    'If some firms close and the survivors hire a few of their workers, a survey of survivors shows jobs “rising.”', 'Which firms can’t answer the second survey?'),
  q(3, 11, 'hard', 'Under World War II wage controls (a maximum wage), what happened to discrimination against minorities and women?', 'It fell: with workers scarce, employers couldn’t turn anyone away', ['It rose: with wages capped, employers hired only people they liked', 'It stayed the same, since wage controls don’t affect who’s hired', 'It disappeared, because wartime laws banned discrimination outright'],
    'When workers are scarce, refusing to hire someone costs the employer dearly. Women and black workers got jobs they hadn’t before.', 'With a maximum wage, were workers scarce or plentiful?'),
  q(3, 11, 'medium', 'How does a minimum wage set above the market rate affect discrimination?', 'It makes discrimination cheaper, with more applicants than jobs', ['It reduces discrimination by giving every worker the same pay', 'It has no effect, since discrimination is about attitudes, not pay', 'It eliminates it, since employers can’t pay some groups less'],
    'A surplus of applicants means turning some away costs nothing, whatever the reason.', 'When there are more applicants than jobs, what does it cost to turn some away?'),
  q(3, 11, 'hard', 'Tiger Wheels expanded in Poland rather than South Africa, where unemployment was 26 percent and output per hour was higher. Why?', 'South African minimum wages and labor rules made hiring unprofitable', ['Polish workers had more skills in making aluminum wheels', 'Poland offered large tax breaks to attract foreign factories', 'Poland was closer to its European suppliers and customers'],
    'It earned a profit in Poland and would only break even or lose money in South Africa, where would-be workers stayed jobless.', 'What made South African workers more expensive to hire than they were productive?'),
  q(3, 12, 'medium', 'What effect have multinational corporations had on wages in China?', 'They paid more than local firms, pushing wages up', ['They pushed wages down by moving to the poorest areas', 'None: wages there are set by the government', 'They raised wages only for their own few employees'],
    'Foreign firms competing for workers raised wages and working conditions, even if they still looked low to Western eyes.', 'Foreign firms had to compete for workers with local ones.'),
  q(3, 12, 'hard', 'In 2010 the share of American adults with jobs fell the most in half a century, yet the unemployment rate didn’t rise. How?', 'People who stopped looking aren’t counted as unemployed', ['Many of the jobless had switched to part-time work', 'The government changed how unemployment was measured', 'Immigrants who lost jobs weren’t counted in the figures'],
    'The unemployment rate only counts people looking for work. Discouraged workers leave the labor force and the statistic.', 'Who counts as unemployed?'),
  q(3, 12, 'medium', 'Why are wages lower in Third World countries?', 'Lower productivity, from less capital and skills', ['Multinationals use their power to keep wages down', 'Workers there have no unions to bargain for them', 'Governments keep wages low to attract investment'],
    'Pay tracks the value of what workers produce. Raise that, and wages follow.', 'Pay follows what workers can produce, with what they have to work with.'),
  q(3, 12, 'hard', 'What tends to happen if a poor country forces wages and working conditions up by law?', 'Fewer jobs and less investment, leaving many worse off', ['Workers earn more and spend more, so the economy grows', 'Employers pay more out of profits, so nothing else changes', 'Productivity rises to match, as better-paid workers work harder'],
    'Employed insiders may gain, but outsiders lose jobs that would have existed. South Africa is a case in point.', 'Employed insiders gain. What about the outsiders?'),

  // ===================================================================
  // Part IV: Time and Risk
  // ===================================================================
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'Does it make sense for a 90-year-old with no heirs to plant trees that take 20 years to bear fruit?', 'Yes: the land is worth more as soon as the trees are planted', ['No: he’ll never see a return on the money and work involved', 'Only for the tax deduction that planting trees can bring', 'Only if he can get a government grant to cover the cost'],
    'Future benefits have a present value. He could sell the land for more as soon as the trees are planted.', 'Could he sell the land, and would the trees change its price?'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'Why do “known reserves” of oil understate how much is in the ground?', 'Proving reserves costs money, so firms only prove what they’ll need', ['Oil companies hide their reserves to keep oil prices high', 'Governments classify reserve figures as state secrets', 'Geologists can only measure oil that’s already been pumped'],
    'Known reserves have risen for decades despite constant use, because exploring further ahead doesn’t pay until it’s needed.', 'Finding and proving oil costs money. How far ahead does it pay to look?'),
  q(4, 13, 'easy', 'Why do “payday loans” have annual interest rates of over 100 percent?', 'Short, small, risky loans have fixed costs, and the fee gets annualized', ['Lenders exploit borrowers who have nowhere else to go for money', 'They aren’t regulated, so lenders charge whatever they like', 'Inflation erodes the money lent faster than for bank loans'],
    'A fee for a two-week loan looks enormous when multiplied out to a full year.', 'What happens when you multiply a two-week fee out to a whole year?'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'How does commodity speculation differ from gambling?', 'Gambling creates risks; speculation takes on existing ones', ['There’s no difference: both are bets on uncertain outcomes', 'Speculators use inside information that gamblers lack', 'Gambling is a game; speculation drives up prices for profit'],
    'Farmers face weather and price risks whatever happens. Speculators take those risks off their hands, for a price.', 'The farmer’s weather risk exists whether or not anyone bets on it.'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'What does commodity speculation do for the allocation of scarce resources?', 'It spreads them over time, from times of plenty to times of need', ['It wastes them, as goods sit in storage waiting for higher prices', 'It raises prices for consumers without adding anything useful', 'Nothing: it only moves money from one speculator to another'],
    'Buying low and selling high means buying when there’s plenty and selling when there’s little.', 'Buy low, sell high: when is a crop cheap, and when is it dear?'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'Can poor, poorly educated Third World farmers benefit from complex international commodity markets?', 'Yes: world prices reach them, through the buyers they sell to', ['No: only big farms with experts can use those markets', 'Only with government agencies negotiating on their behalf', 'No: speculators in those markets drive their prices down'],
    'Farmers don’t need to understand futures contracts any more than drivers need to understand engines.', 'A driver doesn’t need to understand the engine.'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'What kinds of income are called “unearned”?', 'Interest, dividends and rent', ['Inheritances and lottery winnings', 'Welfare payments and pensions', 'Bonuses and tips on top of wages'],
    'They’re payments for waiting and for taking risks. Someone gave up using the money or property now.', 'Income from owning things, rather than from working.'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'What does the interest rate allocate?', 'Resources between now and the future', ['Profits between banks and borrowers', 'Money between rich and poor savers', 'Loans between big and small firms'],
    'Interest is the price of time: it rations resources between people who want them now and uses that pay off later.', 'Interest is the price of time.'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'In 2001 Standard & Poor’s downgraded California’s bonds, though the state paid on time and had billions in surplus. Why?', 'Its electricity-crisis commitments meant taxes or debt were coming', ['Its surplus was about to be spent on new social programs', 'Bond agencies punished it for a political dispute with Wall Street', 'Its bonds were sold mostly to foreigners, which raised the risk'],
    'Bond analysts look past election day. A year later the state’s finances caught up with what they’d seen.', 'Bond analysts look beyond this year’s budget, to the bills coming due.'),
  q(4, 14, 'hard', 'When do individuals raise money with the equivalent of stocks rather than loans?', 'When the venture is very risky, like backing a young boxer', ['When they buy a home with a mortgage from a bank', 'When interest rates are low enough to borrow cheaply', 'When they want to avoid paying taxes on their income'],
    'Most young boxers or actors never make it, so loans would never be repaid. A share of the few winners covers the losers.', 'Who would lend to a young boxer who will probably never make it?'),
  q(4, 14, 'medium', 'Why do both an insurer and the insured come out ahead?', 'Pooled risks shrink, and both sides share the gain', ['Governments subsidize insurers so both can benefit', 'Insurers rarely pay out, so customers just feel safer', 'They don’t: one side always loses what the other gains'],
    'Risk shrinks when it’s pooled. That reduction is what both sides share.', 'Risk shrinks when many risks are pooled together.'),
  q(4, 14, 'medium', 'It makes sense for you to insure your car. Why doesn’t Hertz insure its cars?', 'With so many cars, its losses are predictable enough to cover itself', ['Rental contracts make customers pay for all the damage they cause', 'Rental companies are exempt from the insurance laws drivers face', 'Its cars are new and well maintained, so they rarely get damaged'],
    'A big fleet is already a pool of risks. Insurance would only add the insurer’s costs and profit.', 'One car can crash; a huge fleet has predictable losses.'),
  q(4, 14, 'hard', 'How can government regulation make the insurance industry less efficient?', 'By forcing the same rates for different risks', ['By requiring insurers to keep reserves for claims', 'By licensing agents who sell insurance policies', 'By requiring policies to be written in plain language'],
    'Charge low-risk and high-risk drivers the same and low-risk drivers subsidize the rest, or insurers just leave.', 'What happens if a careful driver and a reckless one must be charged the same?'),
  q(4, 14, 'medium', 'How can government regulation make the insurance industry more efficient?', 'By requiring reserves, so insurers can pay claims', ['By capping premiums so more people can afford cover', 'By banning rates that differ by age or risk', 'By setting one price for everyone, to keep it fair'],
    'Rules that make insurers honest and solvent help. Rules that fight the facts of risk hurt.', 'An insurer is only worth something if it can actually pay when disaster strikes.'),
  q(4, 15, 'hard', 'Why might a government-run bus company set fares too low to replace its buses as they wear out?', 'Low fares win approval now; the bill comes when the buses wear out', ['Public companies don’t pay taxes, so they can afford lower fares', 'Buses last far longer under public ownership, so costs are lower', 'The law requires public transport to be run at cost, never profit'],
    'Politics looks to the next election. The bill for running down the fleet comes due after it.', 'When does the bill for worn-out buses arrive: before or after the next election?'),
  q(4, 15, 'hard', 'If a private bus company’s managers paid themselves more instead of saving for new buses, what would happen?', 'Its stock would fall, and shareholders would replace them', ['Nothing, as long as the buses kept running for now', 'Its stock would rise on the higher reported profits', 'Regulators would step in and take over the company'],
    'The stock price reflects the company’s future, so running down capital shows up now, not years later.', 'A stock price reflects what investors expect the company to be worth in the future.'),
  q(4, 15, 'medium', 'Why has seizing foreign-owned businesses or land seldom made poor countries richer?', 'Much of the value was know-how that left, and investors stayed away', ['The governments that seized them sold them off to cronies cheaply', 'Foreign owners sabotaged the machinery before they left the country', 'The profits were spent on the military instead of on development'],
    'Physical assets don’t run themselves, and confiscation tells future investors to keep out.', 'Can you confiscate the skills of the people who ran the business?'),

  // ===================================================================
  // Part V: The National Economy
  // ===================================================================
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'Why does the consumer price index tend to exaggerate inflation?', 'It misses quality gains, new products and switching to cheaper goods', ['It overweights luxury goods that most people rarely buy', 'It counts temporary price spikes as permanent increases', 'It’s calculated by officials who want higher pension payouts'],
    'A computer that costs the same but does far more is really cheaper. The index can’t see that.', 'A computer that costs the same but does twice as much: did its price really stay the same?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'Why is it hard to say how much national output grew between 1900 and 2000?', 'Much of today’s output didn’t exist then', ['Early records were incomplete and unreliable', 'The dollar has changed value many times since', 'Farm output wasn’t counted until the 1940s'],
    'How do you compare a world without cars, antibiotics or computers to one with them?', 'How many cars or computers were there to count in 1900?'),
  q(5, 16, 'hard', 'During an all-out war, how can military plus civilian consumption exceed a country’s output, without foreign loans?', 'By using up capital: letting equipment, buildings and stocks run down', ['By printing money to pay for both the war and civilian spending', 'By rationing, which makes the same output stretch further', 'By drafting the unemployed, which raises output above normal'],
    'The bill comes later, in worn-out factories, roads and machinery.', 'What happens when machines and buildings aren’t repaired or replaced for years?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'Why is it hard to compare living standards between a young country and an older one?', 'Income rises with age, so younger populations earn less', ['Young people spend more, so they save less for the future', 'Older people’s pensions aren’t counted as part of income', 'Their currencies are measured against different baskets'],
    'Part of what looks like a gap between nations is a gap between age groups.', 'How do people’s incomes change as they get older?'),
  q(5, 16, 'easy', 'What is “the fallacy of composition”?', 'Assuming what’s true of a part is true of the whole', ['Assuming that the whole explains every one of its parts', 'Confusing two things happening together with cause and effect', 'Trusting an argument because of who is making it'],
    'One person standing up at a game sees better. Everyone standing up doesn’t.', 'One person standing up at a game sees better. Does everyone, if all stand?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'Which is an example of the fallacy of composition?', 'Tariffs help one industry, so they must help the economy', ['If prices rise, people will buy less of the product', 'A shortage happens when prices are held down by law', 'Interest is the price people are paid for waiting'],
    'Protection helps one industry at the expense of its customers and other industries.', 'Look for the one that leaps from one part to the whole.'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'During inflation, does money circulate faster or slower?', 'Faster: people spend it before it loses value', ['Slower: people hold on to it, expecting prices to fall', 'The same: inflation changes prices, not spending habits', 'Slower: higher prices mean people can afford less'],
    'Faster spending pushes prices up even further.', 'Would you hold on to money that’s losing value every day?'),
  q(5, 17, 'hard', 'What happens to the circulation of money during deflation?', 'It slows, as people hold money that’s gaining value', ['It speeds up, as lower prices tempt people to buy more', 'It stays the same, since prices fall for everything alike', 'It speeds up, since each dollar now buys more goods'],
    'Holding cash becomes a good investment, so spending, and with it output and jobs, falls further.', 'If money buys more tomorrow than today, when would you spend it?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'Hoover and then Roosevelt both tried to keep up prices and wages in the Great Depression. What was the problem?', 'Prices kept too high leave goods unsold and workers jobless', ['Keeping wages up gave workers more to spend, ending the slump', 'Prices kept up too little, which caused even more deflation', 'It worked, but only after the tariffs of 1930 were repealed'],
    'The aim was to protect purchasing power, but keeping prices up when money is scarce means fewer sales and fewer jobs.', 'What happens to sales when prices are held above what buyers can pay?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'Why would an Albanian bank holding 83 percent of the country’s deposits make no loans at all?', 'Weak laws meant it couldn’t collect from defaulters', ['The government banned private lending after communism', 'It had lent too much before and was nearly bankrupt', 'Interest rates were capped below the rate of inflation'],
    'It bought government securities instead, so capital-hungry businesses were left without finance.', 'What makes lending safe? Think about what happens when a borrower doesn’t pay.'),
  q(5, 18, 'medium', 'How do economic policies affect corruption?', 'More permits and approvals mean more chances for bribes', ['More regulation means more watchdogs, so less corruption', 'They don’t: corruption depends only on a country’s culture', 'Higher official salaries end it, whatever the policies are'],
    'Every official who can say no is an official who can be paid to say yes.', 'Count how many officials must sign off before you can open a shop. Each is a chance for something.'),
  q(5, 18, 'medium', 'Do zoning, open-space laws and height limits affect tenants who own no property?', 'Yes: restricting building pushes up rents', ['No: they only affect people who own land', 'Yes: they lower rents by limiting luxury housing', 'Only tenants of commercial property'],
    'Property rights matter to everyone who pays for housing, not just to those who own it.', 'What happens to rents when less housing can be built?'),
  q(5, 18, 'hard', 'Why might affluent people want housing decisions made by politicians or courts, not the market?', 'They can block building nearby without buying the land', ['Markets ignore the environment, and the wealthy care more', 'They can’t outbid developers in the market for land', 'Courts and politicians are more just than markets'],
    'Open-space laws keep the views and the property values; newcomers pay in higher rents and prices.', 'Buying the land around you is expensive. A vote is cheap.'),
  q(5, 18, 'medium', 'Who helped build Britain’s economy before it led the industrial revolution?', 'Skilled immigrants: Lombard bankers, Flemish weavers, Huguenots', ['Wealth taken from its colonies in India and the Americas', 'Royal monopolies that protected English trades and guilds', 'Native inventors working with English coal and iron'],
    'Medieval Britain lagged behind the continent. Immigrants brought skills Britain didn’t have, and Britain let them work.', 'Medieval Britain lagged behind the continent. Where did the missing skills come from?'),
  q(5, 19, 'medium', 'Why do tax rate increases often bring in less revenue than expected?', 'People earn, invest or report less of what’s taxed', ['Tax collection gets less efficient as rates rise', 'Inflation erodes the value of the taxes collected', 'The rich move all their money abroad overnight'],
    'Tax rates and tax revenues are different things.', 'People can change what they do when it’s taxed more.'),
  q(5, 19, 'hard', 'Why can cuts in tax rates sometimes raise government revenue?', 'Money moves out of tax shelters into taxable uses', ['Lower taxes lead to inflation, which raises revenue', 'The government borrows the difference and counts it', 'People feel richer and voluntarily pay more'],
    'A lower rate on more taxable income can bring in more than a high rate on income hidden in shelters.', 'Where does money hide from high tax rates?'),
  q(5, 19, 'hard', 'When is the burden of the national debt passed on to future generations?', 'When the borrowing pays for current consumption', ['Always: every dollar borrowed is a dollar our children owe', 'Never: we owe it to ourselves, so it cancels out', 'Only when foreigners hold most of the bonds'],
    'Borrowing to build a dam is like a mortgage on a house; borrowing for this year’s spending is living beyond your means.', 'Borrowing for a house and borrowing for a party are different things.'),
  q(5, 19, 'medium', 'Why is it hard to tell who really pays a tax?', 'Taxes get passed on to customers, workers or owners', ['Tax records are confidential and can’t be studied', 'Many people avoid taxes, so the figures are wrong', 'Businesses don’t pay taxes; only individuals do'],
    'Who writes the check isn’t necessarily who bears the cost.', 'Who writes the check isn’t always who bears the cost.'),

  // ===================================================================
  // Part VI: The International Economy
  // ===================================================================
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'How can import restrictions meant to save jobs end up costing jobs?', 'Industries using the protected product pay more and cut jobs', ['Other countries ban our exports in exactly equal measure', 'Protected firms automate to cut their costs even further', 'Workers in protected industries retire early on higher pay'],
    'Steel tariffs cost steel-using industries about 26,000 jobs and $600 million in profits.', 'Who buys the protected product, and what happens to their costs?'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'US restrictions on sugar imports saved jobs in sugar. How many jobs did they cost in candy-making, for each one saved?', 'About three', ['None', 'About half a job', 'About ten'],
    'Some candy makers moved to Canada, Mexico or Guatemala, where sugar was cheaper.', 'Candy makers use a lot of sugar. Some moved abroad, where it was cheaper.'),
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'Country A can make something more cheaply than country B. Why might A still buy it from B?', 'Comparative advantage: A’s resources earn more elsewhere', ['B’s government subsidizes the product to win markets', 'A trade treaty requires A to buy a share from B', 'A wants to help B develop by giving it business'],
    'What matters is what else those resources could have made: the opportunity cost.', 'What else could A’s resources be making instead?'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'Why didn’t prosperous Australia design and build its own cars?', 'Its small market can’t cover the costs of designing cars', ['Its workers are too expensive for car manufacturing', 'Its trade treaties ban it from designing its own cars', 'It lacks the steel and other raw materials needed'],
    'Designing a car costs a fortune. Spreading it over a few hundred thousand buyers makes each car far dearer.', 'Designing a car costs a fortune. How many buyers would share that cost?'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'Why is free trade worth more to producers in a small economy than in a big one?', 'Their home market is too small for economies of scale', ['Small countries have more skilled and educated workers', 'Big countries protect their markets with high tariffs', 'It isn’t: free trade helps big economies far more'],
    'Access to world markets lets small-country firms produce at the scale that keeps costs down.', 'How big can a firm grow selling only at home in a small country?'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'What is a “favorable balance of trade,” and why was it called favorable?', 'Exporting more than importing; mercantilists thought it brought gold', ['Importing more than exporting, since the country gets more goods', 'Trading only with friendly countries, which keeps the peace', 'Exports equal to imports, since neither side loses out'],
    'The name stuck long after the theory behind it was discredited.', 'The mercantilists thought a nation’s wealth was its gold.'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'Is a “favorable” balance of trade actually good for prosperity?', 'Not necessarily: deficits can go with prosperity', ['Yes: exporting more always means more jobs and wealth', 'Yes, but only for small countries that depend on trade', 'No: trade surpluses always lead to recessions later'],
    'The United States has had trade deficits through long periods of prosperity.', 'Is a nation’s wealth its gold, or its goods and services?'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'Which reason for restricting trade do economists usually accept as valid?', 'National defense', ['Saving jobs in an industry', 'Ending a trade deficit', 'Matching low wages abroad'],
    'Most other arguments for protection don’t survive scrutiny.', 'Most arguments for protection fall apart. One involves wars.'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'With free trade, would low-wage countries take away the jobs of high-wage countries?', 'No: what matters is labor cost per unit of output', ['Yes: lower wages always mean lower costs and prices', 'Only factory jobs, while services stay at home', 'Yes, until wages are equal in every country'],
    'If low wages alone decided things, the poorest countries would be the industrial giants.', 'If low wages alone decided it, which countries would be the industrial giants?'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'EU trade restrictions save about 200,000 jobs, at a cost of $43 billion a year. Could it pay each displaced worker $100,000 a year and come out ahead?', 'Yes: each job saved costs about $215,000 a year', ['No: the payments would cost more than the protection', 'It would roughly break even on the numbers given', 'No: it would lose all the taxes those jobs pay'],
    'And the displaced workers could simply find other jobs.', 'Divide $43 billion by 200,000 jobs.'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'What’s a problem with laws against “dumping” (selling abroad below cost)?', 'Costs are hard to pin down, so the laws protect domestic firms', ['Dumping is so rare that the laws are almost never needed', 'They make imports cheaper, which hurts domestic producers', 'They only cover goods, so services can be dumped freely'],
    'Sales, new products and surplus stock are all sold below average cost, by domestic firms too.', 'Do domestic firms ever sell below cost? Think of sales and new products.'),
  q(6, 22, 'hard', 'The US is often a “debtor nation” and Switzerland a “creditor nation.” Is being a debtor nation harmful?', 'Not necessarily: it often means foreigners are investing', ['Yes: it means the country is living beyond its means', 'Yes: it means foreigners will end up owning the economy', 'No: debts between countries always cancel each other out'],
    'Foreigners buying American assets counts as debt, but it reflects confidence in the economy.', 'When foreigners buy American assets, it counts as debt. Why would they buy them?'),
  q(6, 22, 'medium', 'Why does capital seldom flow to the countries where it’s scarcest?', 'Insecure property and corruption make investing there risky', ['Rich countries’ laws forbid most investment in poor ones', 'Transport costs make investment there too expensive', 'Investors don’t know about opportunities in poor countries'],
    'Capital doesn’t flow downhill like water. It flows to where it can earn a return and stay safe.', 'Would you invest where your property might be seized?'),
  q(6, 23, 'medium', 'Africa is more than twice Europe’s size, but Europe has the longer coastline. Why does that matter?', 'Europe’s indented coast has many natural harbors', ['Africa’s coast is more dangerous to sail along', 'Europe has more beaches for fishing villages', 'It doesn’t: coastlines don’t matter today'],
    'Much of Africa’s coastline is smooth, with few good harbors, and many of its rivers aren’t navigable to the sea.', 'A jagged coastline means many sheltered bays. What are those good for?'),
  q(6, 23, 'medium', 'What effect do mountains tend to have on the people who live in them?', 'Isolation and poverty, from costly transport', ['Prosperity from mining the minerals in the mountains', 'Better health and longer lives from the clean air', 'Strong trade, since mountain passes are trade routes'],
    'Mountain peoples around the world have often lagged behind the lowlands below them.', 'How easy is it to trade, or to meet other peoples, from a mountain valley?'),
  q(6, 23, 'hard', 'What do mountains do for the lowlands below them?', 'Supply them with water and fertile soil', ['Block trade and travel to the lowlands', 'Make them colder and harder to farm', 'Nothing: lowlands depend only on rain'],
    'The same mountains that isolate their own people can enrich the valleys and plains below.', 'Rain and melting snow run downhill, carrying soil with them.'),
  q(6, 23, 'medium', 'When Europeans first met the indigenous peoples of the Americas, what gave Europeans a decisive edge?', 'A far larger cultural universe, drawing on all of Eurasia', ['Far more gold and silver to pay for armies', 'A warmer climate that supported bigger populations', 'A natural advantage in intelligence and organization'],
    'Europe borrowed paper, printing, numerals and more from distant peoples. Isolated societies couldn’t draw on such a pool.', 'Paper, printing and numerals reached Europe from distant peoples.'),
  q(6, 23, 'hard', 'How did the two sides’ “disease universes” differ?', 'Europeans had resistance to diseases the Americas had never met', ['Indigenous peoples carried diseases that killed more Europeans', 'Europeans had vaccines and medicines the Americas lacked', 'There was no real difference; war caused most of the deaths'],
    'Diseases killed far more of the indigenous population than warfare did.', 'Diseases from domesticated animals had circulated across Eurasia for centuries.'),
  q(6, 23, 'medium', 'Which of these tends to make a people culturally isolated?', 'Mountains, deserts and few navigable rivers or harbors', ['Many trading ports along a long, open coastline', 'A language spoken widely by neighboring peoples', 'Flat open plains crossed by navigable rivers'],
    'Isolation cuts people off from other peoples’ knowledge, which economic progress depends on.', 'What cuts people off from contact with others?'),

  // ===================================================================
  // Part VII: Special Economic Issues
  // ===================================================================
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'Why can physically identical things sell for different prices?', 'The costs differ: time, place, convenience', ['Sellers charge whatever they can get away with', 'Prices are random, set by habit and guesswork', 'Different taxes apply in different stores'],
    'A drink on a plane and the same drink in a supermarket aren’t the same product, economically.', 'Is a drink on a plane really the same product as one in a supermarket?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'If brands were banned, so you knew what a product was but not who made it, what would happen?', 'Quality would fall, since makers couldn’t be held responsible', ['Prices would fall, since nobody would pay for advertising', 'Quality would rise, as products competed on merit alone', 'Nothing, since products are mostly the same anyway'],
    'Brands are accountability. Even when brands are similar, each maker has a reputation to lose.', 'Without a name, who would you blame for a bad product?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'How does the profit motive help an organization achieve its purpose?', 'It forces it to cut waste, or it loses money', ['It doesn’t: profit distracts from the real purpose', 'Profits pay for better publicity and fundraising', 'Only through the tax breaks that profits bring'],
    'Non-profits can pursue the goals of whoever runs them, at no cost to themselves if they stray.', 'What happens to a business that wastes its resources?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'Before laws against racial discrimination, where were black chemists more likely to be hired?', 'At profit-seeking businesses', ['At non-profit universities', 'In government laboratories', 'At church-run colleges'],
    'Hundreds worked for chemical companies years before non-profit colleges hired black chemistry teachers.', 'Where did turning away a good chemist cost the employer money?'),
  q(7, 24, 'hard', 'How could you test the claim that profits exceed the value of the services businesses provide?', 'See if non-profit or state providers do it cheaper', ['Compare profits to wages and see if profits are too large', 'Survey customers on whether they think prices are fair', 'It can’t be tested; it’s a matter of moral judgment'],
    'If profits were pure waste, profit-free economies should have had lower prices and higher living standards.', 'If profits were pure waste, providers without them should be cheaper. Are they?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'Can government-set prices for medical care reduce its costs?', 'They can lower prices, not costs', ['Yes: lower prices mean lower costs', 'No: they always raise prices', 'Only for patients with insurance'],
    'Price and cost aren’t the same thing.', 'A price and a cost aren’t the same thing.'),
  q(7, 25, 'medium', '“If it saves just one life, it’s worth it.” What’s wrong with that?', 'The money spent could save other lives elsewhere', ['Nothing: a human life is beyond any price at all', 'Safety devices usually fail to work as promised', 'It ignores how many lives are saved by pure luck'],
    'Spending heavily to save one life in one way can cost more lives in ways nobody sees.', 'The same money could be spent saving lives in other ways.'),
  q(7, 25, 'medium', 'Where do natural disasters do the most financial damage, and where do they take the most lives?', 'Money in rich countries; lives in poor ones', ['Most money and most lives in poor countries', 'Most lives in rich, densely built countries', 'Both spread evenly between rich and poor'],
    'Wealth means more property to damage, but also better buildings and help that save lives.', 'Rich countries have more property to lose, and more ways to protect people.'),
  q(7, 25, 'medium', 'An official in India didn’t want multinationals “getting rich selling face creams to poor Indians.” What does that imply?', 'That officials know best what the poor should buy', ['That face creams are harmful to people’s health', 'That multinationals pay too little tax in India', 'That Indian companies make better face creams'],
    'Poor people choosing to buy something is their decision, made with their own money.', 'Who decides what poor people should spend their own money on?'),
  q(7, 26, 'medium', 'How did the mercantilists differ from classical economists like Adam Smith?', 'Mercantilists saw gold as wealth; Smith saw goods and favored free trade', ['Smith wanted export surpluses; mercantilists favored free trade', 'There was no real difference, only in their terms', 'Mercantilists favored free markets; Smith favored planning'],
    'Smith’s Wealth of Nations was largely an attack on mercantilism.', 'Was a nation’s wealth its gold, or what its people could produce?'),
  q(7, 26, 'medium', 'What’s wrong with the theory that prices reflect the amount of labor that goes into things?', 'Prices depend on what buyers value, not on labor', ['Nothing: prices do reflect the labor that goes in', 'It ignores the cost of raw materials and machines', 'Labor is paid too little, so prices are too low'],
    'The theory held for about a century before marginal analysis replaced it.', 'Would lots of labor make a useless thing valuable?'),
  q(7, 26, 'hard', 'Is economics just the ideological opinions of economists?', 'No: economists of opposing ideologies accept the same findings', ['Yes: economists reach whatever conclusions suit their politics', 'Only macroeconomics; microeconomics is settled science', 'Yes, until computers made economic data reliable'],
    'The history of economics shows analysis winning out over the politics of the people doing it.', 'Have economists of opposite politics ever agreed on the analysis?'),
  q(7, 26, 'hard', 'Why is “pass a law to fix this one problem” often a mistake?', 'Everything is connected: fixing one market affects others', ['Laws take years to pass, so problems change in the meantime', 'Most problems fix themselves if left alone long enough', 'Only courts, not lawmakers, can fix economic problems'],
    'General equilibrium theory: you can’t change one price without changing others.', 'In an economy, can you change one price without affecting others?'),
  q(7, 27, 'medium', 'In wartime, why not control the price of bread but leave caviar uncontrolled?', 'Resources would shift out of bread into uncontrolled luxuries', ['Bakers would be forced to bake more, causing a bread surplus', 'Caviar prices would soar, causing inflation across the board', 'No reason not to: it protects the poor at no cost'],
    'Controlling the price of necessities but not luxuries steers resources toward luxuries.', 'If bread pays less and caviar doesn’t, where do farmers and bakers put their effort?'),

  // ===================================================================
  // The basics, from across the book
  // ===================================================================
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'What is “opportunity cost”?', 'The best alternative you give up', ['The price you pay for something', 'The cost of starting a new business', 'The money saved by buying on sale'],
    'Whatever you choose, the real cost is what you could have had instead.', 'Every choice means giving something up.'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'Why does a diamond cost more than water, even though water is essential for life?', 'Scarcity: water is plentiful, diamonds rare', ['Diamonds take far more labor to produce', 'Jewelers control the price of diamonds', 'Water is cheap because governments supply it'],
    'Prices reflect how scarce the next unit is, not how useful the whole supply is.', 'Which one is rare?'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'If its price is free to move, what happens to the price of something that becomes scarcer?', 'It rises', ['It falls', 'It stays the same', 'It’s set by the government'],
    'The higher price tells everyone to use less of it and producers to supply more.', 'When something gets scarcer, people compete harder for it.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'A law caps rents below the market level. What’s the most likely result?', 'A housing shortage', ['A housing surplus', 'Cheaper and better housing', 'A building boom'],
    'More people want apartments at the low rent, and fewer get built or offered.', 'More people want apartments at the low rent, and fewer get offered.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'What is a “black market”?', 'Illegal trading, often above a legal price', ['A market that only trades in luxury goods', 'A sudden crash in the price of stocks', 'Trading in coal, oil and other fuels'],
    'When the legal price is below what people will pay, trading moves underground.', 'What happens to trade the law forbids?'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'Farm price supports keep crop prices above the market level. What do they create?', 'Surpluses', ['Shortages', 'Cheaper food', 'Nothing in particular'],
    'At the higher price, farmers grow more and consumers buy less: the difference is a surplus.', 'At a higher price, farmers grow more and people buy less.'),
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'What happens to most new businesses?', 'They fail within a few years', ['They become big corporations', 'They become monopolies', 'They’re taken over by the government'],
    'Business is a process of trial and error. The ones we hear about are the survivors.', 'The businesses we hear about are the survivors.'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'How do businesses earn profits in a free market?', 'By serving customers better or more cheaply', ['By paying workers less than their work is worth', 'By getting favors and protection from government', 'By charging as much as customers will tolerate'],
    'Customers who can go elsewhere only pay if they get something worth it.', 'Customers can always go elsewhere.'),
  q(3, 10, 'easy', 'What mainly decides how much an employer will pay a worker?', 'The value of what that worker produces', ['How much the worker needs to live on', 'What the law and unions decide is fair', 'How long the worker has been employed'],
    'An employer who pays more than a worker brings in loses money; one who pays much less loses the worker to a rival.', 'An employer can’t pay more than a worker brings in, for long.'),
  q(3, 11, 'easy', 'Who tends to be hit hardest by minimum wage laws?', 'Young, inexperienced, low-skilled workers', ['Owners of large corporations and their shareholders', 'Skilled tradesmen and craftsmen', 'Retirees living on their pensions'],
    'Their work is worth the least to employers so far, so they’re the first priced out of jobs, and out of the experience that would raise their pay.', 'Whose work is worth the least to employers so far?'),
  q(4, 14, 'easy', 'Why do stocks usually earn a higher average return than bonds?', 'Stocks are riskier, so investors need more', ['Bonds are taxed more heavily than stocks', 'Companies must pay stockholders first', 'Stock markets are less regulated than bonds'],
    'Bondholders are paid first, a fixed amount. Stockholders get what’s left, if anything.', 'Stockholders get what’s left over, if anything.'),
  q(4, 14, 'easy', 'What does an insurance company actually sell?', 'Protection against risk', ['Investments that grow over time', 'Loans to people after an accident', 'Guaranteed savings for retirement'],
    'Few houses burn down in a given year, so many small premiums cover the few big losses.', 'Few houses burn down in a year. Many owners worry that theirs will.'),
  q(5, 16, 'easy', 'What does Gross Domestic Product (GDP) measure?', 'A country’s total output of goods and services', ['The total amount the government spends each year', 'The amount of money circulating in the economy', 'The average income earned by workers in a country'],
    'It’s a measure of output, not of money or of what the government spends.', 'It’s about what a country produces.'),
  q(5, 17, 'easy', 'What is inflation?', 'A general rise in prices, as money loses value', ['A rise in the price of an important product', 'Growth in the economy’s total output', 'A rise in wages faster than productivity'],
    'One price rising is a change in relative prices. All prices rising is inflation.', 'It’s about the value of money, not of one product.'),
  q(5, 17, 'easy', 'If the government prints a trillion dollars and hands it to everyone, what happens?', 'Inflation: more money chasing the same goods', ['Everyone gets richer and spends more', 'The economy grows as spending rises', 'Nothing, if the money is shared equally'],
    'Printing money doesn’t print goods. The same output just costs more.', 'Printing money doesn’t print goods.'),
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'Free trade between two countries generally…', 'Benefits both sides', ['Makes both poorer', 'Only helps the richer one', 'Causes war'],
    'Each side trades because it gets something it values more than what it gives up.', 'Why would either side trade if it didn’t gain?'),
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'What does a tariff do to the price of imported goods?', 'Raises it', ['Lowers it', 'Leaves it unchanged', 'Bans them entirely'],
    'A tariff is a tax on imports, and domestic buyers end up paying it.', 'A tariff is a tax on imports.'),
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'Who pays most of the cost of a tariff?', 'Domestic consumers, through higher prices', ['The foreign country’s exporters', 'The foreign government that’s targeted', 'Nobody: the tariff is a free gain'],
    'The protected industry gains; everyone who buys its products, or products made from them, pays.', 'Who buys the imported goods?'),
  q(7, 26, 'easy', 'Who wrote The Wealth of Nations, published in 1776?', 'Adam Smith', ['Karl Marx', 'John Maynard Keynes', 'Milton Friedman'],
    'Smith launched classical economics with it, attacking the mercantilists.', 'A Scottish professor of moral philosophy.'),
  q(7, 26, 'easy', 'What did Adam Smith’s The Wealth of Nations argue for?', 'Free markets and free trade', ['Central planning', 'Hoarding gold through export surpluses', 'Absolute monarchy'],
    'Smith argued that the wealth of a nation is its goods and services, not its gold.', 'Smith attacked the mercantilists.'),
  // ===================================================================
  // More from the chapters: core ideas (easy) and Sowell’s cases (hard)
  // ===================================================================

  // --- Part I ---
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Scarcity is one thing at the heart of economics. What’s the other, according to Sowell?', 'Alternative uses', ['Money and prices', 'Self-interest', 'Government policy'],
    'If each resource had only one possible use, economics would be much simpler. Water alone can become ice, steam or countless mixtures.', 'Water can become ice, steam, or a thousand mixtures.'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Japan and Switzerland have few natural resources but high living standards. What does that show?', 'How resources are used matters more than how many there are', ['Small, cold countries tend to be richer than big, warm ones', 'They import all their wealth from resource-rich countries', 'Having few resources forces people to work much harder'],
    'Economic decisions about land, labor and capital can matter more than the resources themselves.', 'If resources made countries rich, which countries would be richest?'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Uruguay and Venezuela have several times more natural resources per person than Japan and Switzerland. How do their incomes compare?', 'Japan and Switzerland’s are double Uruguay’s and several times Venezuela’s', ['Uruguay and Venezuela’s are double Japan’s and several times Switzerland’s', 'All four are about equal once living costs are counted', 'Venezuela’s is the highest of the four, thanks to its oil'],
    'Riches in the ground don’t make a country rich. What it does with what it has does.', 'Riches in the ground don’t guarantee riches above it.'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'When the price of something rises, what usually happens to the quantity people buy?', 'It falls', ['It rises', 'It stays exactly the same', 'It drops to zero'],
    'People economize on what costs more and turn to substitutes.', 'People economize on what costs more.'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'What do prices do in a market economy?', 'Carry information and incentives', ['Reveal how greedy sellers are', 'Reflect what experts decide', 'Measure what is fair to charge'],
    'A higher price tells everyone a thing has become scarcer, and makes them use less of it, without anyone knowing why.', 'A price rise tells you something, and makes you do something about it.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'In the 1960s Ghana was richer than its neighbor the Ivory Coast, with more natural resources. What had happened by 1982?', 'The freer-market Ivory Coast was far ahead', ['Ghana’s resources and planning had made it far richer', 'Both had collapsed equally after independence', 'They were about equal, as neighbors usually are'],
    'Later, when the Ivory Coast added controls and Ghana loosened them, their fortunes reversed again.', 'One chose a government-run economy, the other a freer market.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'India and South Korea were at similar economic levels in 1960. Where were they by the late 1980s?', 'South Korea’s was ten times India’s', ['India’s was twice South Korea’s', 'They were still about the same', 'South Korea’s was a little ahead'],
    'India stuck with a government-controlled economy for decades after independence; South Korea freed its economy far earlier.', 'One freed its economy decades before the other.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'In the 1990s India dropped four decades of economic planning and isolation. What happened to its growth rate?', 'It rose to about 6 percent a year', ['It fell into years of recession', 'It stayed at its old slow rate', 'It fell by about half at first'],
    'From 1950 to 1990, India’s growth had been far slower. Freeing its entrepreneurs changed that.', 'The Economist called it one of the world’s fastest-growing big economies.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'Under price controls, if price doesn’t ration scarce goods, what does?', 'Lines, favoritism and black markets', ['Fair lotteries run by the government', 'Need, as judged by impartial officials', 'Nothing: controls make enough for all'],
    'Something always has to decide who gets what’s scarce. Remove prices and other, often uglier, methods take over.', 'Something always decides who gets what’s scarce. Think of the Soviet lines.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'After a disaster, higher prices for things like water and lumber encourage what?', 'Suppliers to rush in more, and people to use only what they need', ['Businesses to hold back supplies until prices rise even more', 'People to panic and buy far more than they actually need', 'Nothing useful: they just make the disaster more profitable'],
    'High prices pull supplies toward the disaster area and stop the first comers from emptying the shelves.', 'High prices pull supplies in, and stop the first comers emptying the shelves.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'The 1906 San Francisco earthquake and fire destroyed over half the city’s housing. Was there a housing shortage?', 'No: rents rose, and offers of housing outnumbered seekers', ['Yes: thousands lived in tents for more than a decade', 'Yes, until the state built emergency housing', 'No, because almost everyone left the city'],
    'Rising rents got people to share, take in roomers and rebuild. Physical scarcity grew, but there was no shortage.', 'Rents were free to rise. Did people share, take in roomers and rebuild?'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'Consumer Reports tested candy bars in 1943, under World War II price controls. What did it find?', '19 out of 20 had shrunk since four years earlier', ['Most had doubled in price despite the controls', 'They were exactly the same as four years earlier', 'Most had gotten bigger to compete for customers'],
    'When the price can’t rise, the product can quietly shrink or get worse instead.', 'If the price can’t go up, the product can quietly change.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'Nine years after World War II, how many new apartment buildings had been built in rent-controlled Melbourne, Australia?', 'None at all', ['Hundreds', 'A few dozen', 'Thousands'],
    'Rent control made new apartment buildings unprofitable. Egypt, after its 1960 rent control, saw the same thing.', 'Would you build an apartment block whose rents were held below cost?'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'New York City has taken over thousands of abandoned buildings. How do abandoned housing units compare with the city’s street homeless?', 'At least four times as many abandoned units as street homeless', ['About half as many abandoned units as street homeless', 'About the same number of each, which is why it’s a crisis', 'Almost no abandoned units: they were all demolished'],
    'Homelessness there isn’t a physical scarcity of housing but a price-related shortage, though no less real for that.', 'Homelessness there isn’t a physical shortage of buildings.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'In 2007 Zimbabwe ordered sellers to cut prices in half or more to fight runaway inflation. What happened a month later?', 'The economy halted and staples vanished', ['Inflation ended and prices stayed down', 'Prices halved and shops stayed well stocked', 'Imports surged as people could afford more'],
    'The further a controlled price is from the market price, the worse the consequences.', 'Would you keep selling bread at half what it cost you?'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'How do laws against “price gouging” affect supplies before a forecast hurricane?', 'Less gets rushed in, since higher prices can’t cover the extra costs', ['More gets shipped in, since suppliers know they’ll be treated fairly', 'No effect: suppliers ship the same amount whatever the law says', 'The government takes over, shipping everything in at normal prices'],
    'Hurricanes are usually forecast. Expected higher prices are what get flashlights, water and lumber there before the storm.', 'Rushing goods toward a hurricane costs extra. What covers that cost?'),
  q(1, 4, 'easy', 'When judging an economic policy, what matters most, according to Sowell?', 'Its actual results, not its aims', ['The goals it sets out to achieve', 'Who designed it and their motives', 'How many voters support it'],
    'Good intentions don’t guarantee good results. Economics asks what incentives a policy creates and what follows from them.', 'Good intentions don’t guarantee good results.'),

  // --- Part II ---
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'In 1976 Kodak sold 90 percent of the film in the US; in 2012 it filed for bankruptcy. What happened?', 'Digital cameras and smartphones replaced film', ['Anti-trust regulators broke up the company', 'Japanese film makers dumped cheap film in the US', 'A long strike shut down its factories for years'],
    'New technology brought new competitors, from camera makers to Sony, Samsung and phone makers.', 'Do you still buy rolls of film?'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'What was the ultimate irony of Kodak’s fall?', 'Kodak had invented the digital camera', ['Kodak had turned down buying Apple', 'Kodak’s film sales were still rising', 'Kodak had sold its camera unit to Sony'],
    'Inventing the future isn’t enough if others make better use of it.', 'The technology that ruined it came from an unexpected place.'),
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'What’s the main lesson of businesses rising and falling over time?', 'Firms must keep adapting or lose their customers', ['Big businesses last forever once they get big', 'Only small firms fail; big ones get bailed out', 'Governments decide which businesses survive'],
    'Between 2010 and 2011 alone, 26 businesses dropped off the Fortune 500, including Radio Shack.', '26 companies dropped off the Fortune 500 in a single year.'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'As Standard Oil came to sell about 90 percent of the country’s kerosene, what happened to its price?', 'It fell from 58 cents a gallon to about 8 cents', ['It rose from 8 cents a gallon to about 58 cents', 'It stayed at about 26 cents a gallon throughout', 'It doubled each time Standard Oil bought a rival'],
    'For the first time, working people could afford light after dark: about a cent an hour.', 'For the first time, working people could afford light after dark.'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'What are economies of scale?', 'Lower costs per unit at larger output', ['Higher prices charged for bigger sizes', 'Savings from a smaller government', 'Discounts for buying in small amounts'],
    'Making 100,000 cars a year costs far less per car than making 100.', 'Making 100,000 cars costs far less per car than making 100.'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'How did Henry Ford make cars affordable for ordinary Americans?', 'Mass production cut the cost of each car', ['Government subsidies for car buyers', 'Using cheaper, lower-quality parts', 'Selling cars at a loss to win buyers'],
    'His methods spread throughout the economy, not just the car industry.', 'Think about how many cars he made, and what that did to the cost of each.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'What happened to the price of a Model T Ford between 1910 and 1916?', 'It was cut in half', ['It doubled', 'It stayed the same', 'It tripled'],
    'Ford kept improving his factories. The time to build a Model T chassis shrank dramatically.', 'Ford kept making his factories more efficient.'),
  q(2, 7, 'easy', 'What does “limited liability” mean for a stockholder?', 'You can lose what you put in, but no more', ['The company can’t be sued by its customers', 'Your share of the profits is capped by law', 'You pay a lower rate of tax on dividends'],
    'That’s why corporations can raise money from millions of investors who never see the business.', 'It’s what “Ltd.” and “Inc.” are about.'),
  q(2, 8, 'easy', 'What was the Interstate Commerce Commission, created in 1887, set up to regulate?', 'Railroads', ['Airlines', 'Telephones', 'Television'],
    'It was the first of many federal regulatory commissions, and later extended its reach to trucking.', 'In 1887, what was the big way to move freight across the country?'),
  q(2, 8, 'hard', 'Why did the Interstate Commerce Commission make truckers get certificates to cross state lines?', 'To protect the railroads from truck competition', ['To keep unsafe trucks off interstate highways', 'To collect taxes for building the new highways', 'To protect small truckers from big railroads'],
    'A commission created to keep railroads in check ended up protecting them.', 'Trucks were cheaper for many kinds of freight. Who stood to lose?'),
  q(2, 8, 'hard', 'What happened when trucking was deregulated in 1980?', 'Freight charges fell and service improved', ['Prices rose as unregulated firms took over', 'The trucking industry collapsed into chaos', 'The railroads went bankrupt within a decade'],
    'Fewer trucks drove around empty, and dependable deliveries let businesses carry smaller inventories, saving tens of billions.', 'Fewer trucks drove around empty afterwards.'),
  q(2, 8, 'hard', 'Under strict Texas regulation, shipping jeans from El Paso to Dallas cost about 40 percent more than shipping them from where?', 'From Taiwan to Dallas', ['From New York to Dallas', 'From Mexico City', 'From London'],
    'Interstate shipping had been deregulated; shipping within Texas hadn’t.', 'Shipping within Texas was still strictly regulated. International shipping wasn’t.'),
  q(2, 8, 'hard', 'What happened after the Civil Aeronautics Board, which regulated airlines, was abolished?', 'Fares fell and far more people flew', ['Fares rose as airlines cut unprofitable routes', 'Fewer people flew as safety standards slipped', 'One big airline took over most of the market'],
    'Planes also flew with more of their seats filled. It wasn’t zero-sum: the industry became more efficient.', 'Planes started flying with more of their seats filled.'),
  q(2, 9, 'easy', 'What’s the key difference between a business and a non-market producer like a government agency?', 'A business must cover its costs from willing customers', ['Businesses never make mistakes; agencies often do', 'Agencies cost less because they don’t make profits', 'There’s no real difference in how they operate'],
    'An agency funded by taxes can keep going whether or not people value what it does.', 'Who pays if an agency wastes money, and what happens to it?'),

  // --- Part III ---
  q(3, 10, 'easy', 'What raises workers’ pay over the long run, most of all?', 'Higher productivity', ['Minimum wage laws', 'Union bargaining', 'Rising prices'],
    'Workers who produce more are worth more to employers, who compete for them.', 'Workers who produce more are worth more to employers.'),
  q(3, 11, 'easy', 'Which of these countries had no minimum wage law, and low unemployment, when Sowell wrote?', 'Switzerland', ['France', 'South Africa', 'Spain'],
    'Its cabinet rejected a minimum wage in 2013, with unemployment at 3.1 percent.', 'Its cabinet rejected a minimum wage in 2013.'),
  q(3, 11, 'hard', 'Singapore, and Hong Kong in 1991, had no minimum wage law. What were their unemployment rates?', 'About 2 percent', ['About 10 percent', 'About 25 percent', 'About 15 percent'],
    'Under Coolidge, before any federal minimum wage, US unemployment got as low as 1.8 percent.', 'Lower than most countries with a minimum wage.'),
  q(3, 11, 'hard', 'In 1948, before minimum wages started rising repeatedly, how did black and white teenage unemployment compare?', 'Similar, around 10 percent, far below later rates', ['Black teen unemployment was about three times higher', 'Both were above 40 percent, as in later decades', 'Black teens had almost no unemployment at all'],
    'Even in the 1949 recession, black teen unemployment was lower than it would be in the boom years of the 1960s.', 'Black teenage unemployment soared only in later decades.'),
  q(3, 12, 'easy', 'Job security laws make it hard to fire workers. What do they tend to do to unemployment?', 'Raise it, as employers hire less', ['Lower it, as fewer people get fired', 'Nothing: they just protect workers', 'Eliminate it in the long run'],
    'Every hire becomes a risk the employer can’t easily undo.', 'Would you hire someone you could never let go?'),
  q(3, 12, 'hard', 'France has some of Europe’s strongest job security laws. What has its unemployment been like?', 'Double-digit rates are common', ['Among the lowest in Europe', 'Usually under 2 percent', 'About the same as the US'],
    'Countries with strong job protection typically have higher unemployment and longer spells out of work.', 'Americans get alarmed when unemployment nears that level.'),
  q(3, 12, 'easy', 'What is occupational licensing?', 'Needing government permission to work in a trade', ['A card showing membership in a trade union', 'A license required to drive for a living', 'A special tax paid by self-employed workers'],
    'Licenses can protect consumers, but they also limit who can compete, raising prices.', 'Think barbers, plumbers and taxi drivers.'),
  q(3, 12, 'hard', 'Child labor laws were passed to protect children from dangerous factories. According to an observer Sowell quotes, what do they do now?', 'Keep teenagers out of air-conditioned offices', ['Still protect children in mines and mills', 'Nothing: they were all repealed long ago', 'Guarantee teenagers higher wages than adults'],
    'Laws passed for one set of conditions often stay on the books long after the conditions change.', 'Laws often outlive the conditions they were written for.'),

  // --- Part IV ---
  q(4, 13, 'easy', 'What is “present value”?', 'What future benefits are worth today', ['The price something sells for today', 'The value of a gift at the time it’s given', 'The value set by a tax assessor each year'],
    'It’s why the land the old man plants trees on becomes worth more right away.', 'A promise of money later is worth something now.'),
  q(4, 13, 'easy', 'What does a speculator do?', 'Takes on price risks, hoping to profit', ['Hoards goods to create shortages', 'Sets prices for an entire market', 'Lends money at high interest rates'],
    'Speculation is risky for the speculator, but it reduces risks for the economy as a whole.', 'Someone has to carry the risk that prices will change.'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'In 1960 a best-seller said the US had only a 13-year supply of oil. What were the known reserves by 1973?', 'Larger than in 1960', ['Zero: the oil had run out', 'About half of the 1960 level', 'Exactly what was predicted'],
    'The arithmetic was right. But known reserves are what it pays to prove, not what’s in the ground.', 'Known reserves are what it pays to prove, not what’s in the ground.'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'In the 1870s Henry Heinz contracted to buy farmers’ cucumbers at fixed prices. What happened?', 'A bumper crop he couldn’t afford bankrupted him', ['Prices soared, and his fixed contracts made him rich', 'Farmers broke the contracts and went bankrupt', 'The government bought the surplus cucumbers'],
    'He took on the farmers’ price risk, and lost. Years later he started over and founded the H.J. Heinz company.', 'He promised to buy, whatever the size of the crop.'),
  q(4, 14, 'easy', 'Stocks or bonds: which pays a fixed, guaranteed return?', 'Bonds', ['Stocks', 'Both', 'Neither'],
    'Bondholders are creditors, paid first. Stockholders own what’s left over, if anything.', 'Lenders are paid a fixed amount; owners get what’s left.'),
  q(4, 14, 'easy', 'What is a futures contract?', 'A deal to buy or sell later at a price set now', ['A government bond that pays out in the future', 'A lottery ticket on what prices will be', 'A promise of a raise in future wages'],
    'It shifts the risk of price changes from one party to another.', 'Heinz signed them with cucumber farmers.'),

  // --- Part V ---
  q(5, 16, 'easy', 'In the Great Depression, US unemployment went from 3 percent in 1929 to what in 1933?', '25 percent', ['10 percent', '50 percent', '5 percent'],
    'It was the greatest economic catastrophe in the country’s history.', 'About one worker in four.'),
  q(5, 16, 'hard', 'How much lower was America’s real output in 1933 than in 1929?', 'About one-fourth lower', ['About 10 percent higher', 'Half as large', 'About the same'],
    'Stock prices plunged and corporations as a whole lost money two years running.', 'Corporations as a whole lost money two years in a row.'),
  q(5, 17, 'easy', 'What is money’s main function?', 'A medium of exchange that makes trade easier', ['A store of gold kept for the nation’s safety', 'A way for the government to raise revenue', 'A measure of how much a person is worth'],
    'Without money, every trade would need two people who each want what the other has.', 'Without it, you’d need someone who wants what you have and has what you want.'),
  q(5, 17, 'easy', 'What do banks do with most of the money deposited with them?', 'Lend it out', ['Keep it all in their vaults', 'Hand it to the government', 'Destroy old bills'],
    'Banks channel savings from people who don’t need the money now to people who can use it.', 'How else would a bank pay interest?'),
  q(5, 17, 'hard', 'In Germany, 40 marks bought a dollar in July 1920. How many did it take by November 1923?', 'More than 4 trillion', ['More than 4 million', 'More than 4 billion', 'More than 40 thousand'],
    'People found their life’s savings wouldn’t buy a pack of cigarettes. Inflation is a hidden tax.', 'Life savings wouldn’t buy a pack of cigarettes.'),
  q(5, 18, 'easy', 'Which of these is a basic job of government that a market economy needs?', 'Enforcing property rights and contracts', ['Setting fair prices for basic goods', 'Running the most important industries', 'Deciding what everyone should be paid'],
    'Markets work best on a framework of rules that let people keep what they earn and rely on agreements.', 'Markets need rules that let people keep what they earn.'),
  q(5, 18, 'easy', 'What is an “external cost”?', 'A cost imposed on outsiders, like pollution', ['The extra cost of buying imported goods', 'A tax paid to a foreign government', 'The cost of hiring outside contractors'],
    'Since the parties to the deal don’t pay it, they have no reason to weigh it.', 'It falls on people who weren’t part of the deal.'),
  q(5, 19, 'easy', 'A tax that takes a higher percentage from higher incomes is called…', 'Progressive', ['Regressive', 'Flat', 'A sales tax'],
    'A tax that takes a higher percentage from lower incomes is regressive.', 'The rate progresses upward with income.'),
  q(5, 20, 'easy', 'Market failure is a reason governments give for stepping in. What’s Sowell’s point about it?', 'Governments fail too; compare which works better', ['Markets never fail if left completely alone', 'Governments never fail if experts run them', 'Market failure is a myth invented by economists'],
    'Comparing a real market with an ideal government is no comparison at all.', 'Compare a real market with a real government, not an ideal one.'),
  q(5, 20, 'hard', 'Unemployment hit 11.7 percent in 1921 under President Harding. What did he do, and what followed?', 'He cut spending, and unemployment soon fell to 2.4 percent', ['He launched big public works, and unemployment fell', 'He imposed price and wage controls to stop the slide', 'He did nothing, and unemployment stayed high for years'],
    'The opposite of what Keynesian economists would later advise, and the downturn ended within two years.', 'He did the opposite of what Keynesians would later advise.'),
  q(5, 20, 'hard', 'How many economists signed a public statement warning against the Smoot-Hawley tariffs of 1930?', 'About a thousand', ['About ten', 'About a hundred', 'None'],
    'They predicted the tariffs would fail to cut unemployment and hurt farm exports. They were right.', 'Economists at leading universities around the country.'),
  q(5, 20, 'hard', 'What has let Social Security postpone its day of reckoning?', 'A small 1930s generation was followed by the bigger baby boom', ['Its trust fund invested in stocks that grew over the decades', 'Its trust fund holds real assets that pay for future pensions', 'Benefits were cut a little every year to keep it balanced'],
    'Its trust fund holds government bonds: promises to pay from future taxpayers, not tangible wealth.', 'Each generation’s pensions are paid by the next generation’s taxes.'),

  // --- Part VI ---
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'Who first explained comparative advantage, in his Principles of Political Economy of 1817?', 'David Ricardo', ['Adam Smith', 'John Maynard Keynes', 'Karl Marx'],
    'Ricardo was the leading economist of the early nineteenth century.', 'The leading economist of the early 1800s, and a member of Parliament.'),
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'What did the Smoot-Hawley Act of 1930 do?', 'Raised US tariffs to their highest in a century', ['Cut income taxes to fight the Depression', 'Created the Federal Reserve to steady banks', 'Set the first federal minimum wage'],
    'The aim was to protect American jobs during the Depression.', 'It was meant to protect American jobs from imports.'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'Unemployment was 6 percent when the Smoot-Hawley tariffs passed in June 1930. What was it two years later?', '26 percent', ['3 percent', '10 percent', 'Still 6 percent'],
    'Not all of that was due to the tariffs, but the whole point of them was to reduce unemployment. US farm exports plummeted too.', 'It got much worse, not better.'),
  q(6, 22, 'easy', 'What are remittances?', 'Money migrants send home to family', ['Foreign aid paid by governments', 'Tariffs refunded to exporters', 'Payments on a country’s debts'],
    'In 2012, 250 million migrants sent home $410 billion.', 'Think of migrants and the families they left behind.'),
  q(6, 22, 'hard', 'In 2011, about what share of Mexicans received money from family members in the United States?', 'About one in five', ['About 1 percent', 'About half', 'Nearly everyone'],
    'Nearly $23 billion in total. In some countries remittances are over a third of GDP.', 'Nearly $23 billion in total, spread across a country of 112 million.'),
  q(6, 22, 'easy', 'Which postwar aid program helped Western Europe recover after World War II?', 'The Marshall Plan', ['The New Deal', 'The Smoot-Hawley Act', 'The Truman Doctrine of trade'],
    'It worked far better than many later attempts to copy it in the Third World.', 'Named after a US Secretary of State.'),
  q(6, 22, 'hard', 'Why did the Marshall Plan succeed in Western Europe when later aid to Third World countries often didn’t?', 'Europe already had an industrial society’s skills; aid helped it rebuild', ['It was far bigger than any aid given to Third World countries since', 'It came with trade rules that protected European industry from imports', 'Europe had more natural resources than most Third World countries'],
    'Physical capital is of little use without the skills and experience to use it.', 'Europe had pioneered industry before the war. What did it still have afterwards?'),
  q(6, 22, 'hard', 'In Peru, how valuable is the real estate that isn’t covered by legal property rights?', 'Over a dozen times all foreign investment there', ['Almost nothing, since most of it is in slums', 'About as much as one year of foreign aid', 'About half of Peru’s annual output'],
    'Poor countries often have vast wealth that can’t be used as collateral. In Egypt, some 4.7 million homes were built illegally.', 'Hernando de Soto found vast wealth that couldn’t be used as collateral.'),
  q(6, 23, 'easy', 'Why are most of the world’s great cities on rivers, lakes or harbors?', 'Water transport is far cheaper than land', ['People needed the fresh water to drink', 'Rivers gave natural lines of defense', 'Waterfront land was the most fertile'],
    'Cities need huge amounts of food and fuel brought in, every day.', 'Cities need huge amounts of food and fuel brought in every day.'),
  q(6, 23, 'hard', 'In 1830, cargo that cost over $30 to ship 300 miles over land could be shipped 3,000 miles across the Atlantic for how much?', 'About $10', ['About $300', 'About $3,000', 'About $30'],
    'Which is why access to navigable waterways has shaped where people prospered.', 'Water transport was far cheaper than land transport.'),
  q(6, 23, 'hard', 'Huguenots fleeing persecution in France took watchmaking skills to which countries, making them leading watchmakers?', 'England and Switzerland', ['Germany and Austria', 'Italy and Spain', 'Belgium and the Netherlands'],
    'France faced new competition in industries it had dominated, from the people it had driven out.', 'One became famous for clocks, the other for London watchmaking.'),

  // --- Part VII ---
  q(7, 24, 'easy', 'Why might you pay more at a hotel with a famous brand name?', 'You know what you’ll get without having to find out', ['Brand names are a status symbol, nothing more', 'Famous hotels pay higher taxes and pass them on', 'Famous brands are always in the best locations'],
    'A brand is a reputation on the line, and the reputation is worth protecting.', 'A brand is a reputation on the line.'),
  q(7, 25, 'easy', 'According to Sowell, what has saved more lives than almost anything else?', 'The growth of wealth', ['Government regulation', 'Price controls', 'Foreign aid'],
    'Wealth pays for stronger buildings, better hospitals, clean water and sewers.', 'It pays for stronger buildings, better hospitals and clean water.'),
  q(7, 25, 'hard', 'An earthquake strong enough to kill a dozen people in California would kill how many in a Third World nation?', 'Thousands', ['About a dozen', 'Fewer', 'None at all'],
    'Wealthy places build structures that withstand far greater stresses, and get the injured to hospitals faster.', 'Rich places build structures that withstand far greater stresses.'),
  q(7, 25, 'hard', 'In 2013, where were natural disasters’ biggest financial costs, and their biggest costs in lives?', 'Money: Germany, Czech Republic, France. Lives: Philippines, India', ['Money: Philippines, India. Lives: Germany, Czech Republic, France', 'Both the money and the lives: the Philippines and India', 'Both the money and the lives: Germany, France and Italy'],
    'Rich countries have more property to lose but far fewer people die.', 'Rich countries lose more property; poor countries lose more people.'),
  q(7, 26, 'easy', 'Who were the “classical economists”?', 'Adam Smith, David Ricardo and their successors', ['The philosophers of ancient Greece and Rome', 'The mercantilists who advised European kings', 'Keynes and the economists who followed him'],
    'They believed relative prices mostly reflected costs of production, until the 1870s brought a new approach.', 'The school that ran from The Wealth of Nations to the 1870s.'),
  q(7, 26, 'hard', 'Who, in the 1870s, explained prices by the utility of goods to consumers?', 'Carl Menger and W. Stanley Jevons', ['Adam Smith and David Ricardo', 'Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels', 'John Maynard Keynes and Friedrich Hayek'],
    'Menger in Austria and Jevons in England, independently, launched what became modern economics.', 'One was in Austria, the other in England.'),
  q(7, 26, 'hard', 'What did Menger and Jevons see about diamonds and water that Adam Smith missed?', 'Prices depend on the value of one more unit', ['Diamonds are actually more useful than water', 'Prices depend on the labor needed to make things', 'Water should be priced by need, not by markets'],
    'Smith compared total utility: life without water vs. life without diamonds. The question that sets prices is about the next gallon or the next diamond.', 'Is the next gallon of water worth as much as the first?'),
  q(7, 26, 'hard', 'What popular fear did Say’s Law answer?', 'That output could outgrow people’s ability to buy it', ['That prices would keep rising and never come down', 'That money would run out as the economy grew', 'That free trade would lead to wars between nations'],
    'Named for Jean-Baptiste Say, it was fought over in the 1800s and again after Keynes in 1936.', 'People feared the economy would produce more than anyone could buy.'),
] as const;

